!deck: Math Identities
!tags: Math Trigonometric Hyperbolic Identity
!type: Cloze Math

!id: zvAyOSCTQW
!text:
\[
{{c2::e^{ix}::\ldots}}
=
{{c1::\cos x + i \sin x::\ldots}}
\]

Euler's Formula
!back extra:
\[
\begin{align*}
e^{ix} &= 1 + ix + \frac{(ix)^2}{2!} + \frac{(ix)^3}{3!} + \frac{(ix)^4}{4!} + \frac{(ix)^5}{5!} + \ldots \\
&= 1 + ix - \frac{x^2}{2!} - \frac{ix^3}{3!} + \frac{x^4}{4!} + \frac{ix^5}{5!} + \ldots \\
&= \left( 1 - \frac{x^2}{2!} + \frac{x^4}{4!} - \ldots \right) + i \left( x - \frac{x^3}{3!} + \frac{x^5}{5!} - \ldots \right) \\
&= \cos(x) + i \sin(x)
\end{align*}
\]
~~~

!id: Rjr9k6j6ba
!text:
\[
{{c2::e^{-ix}::\ldots}}
=
{{c1::\cos x - i \sin x::\ldots}}
\]

Euler's Formula
!back extra:
\[
\begin{align*}
e^{-ix} &= 1 + (-ix) + \frac{(-ix)^2}{2!} + \frac{(-ix)^3}{3!} + \frac{(-ix)^4}{4!} + \frac{(-ix)^5}{5!} + \ldots \\
&= 1 - ix - \frac{x^2}{2!} + \frac{ix^3}{3!} + \frac{x^4}{4!} - \frac{ix^5}{5!} + \ldots \\
&= \left( 1 - \frac{x^2}{2!} + \frac{x^4}{4!} - \ldots \right) - i \left( x - \frac{x^3}{3!} + \frac{x^5}{5!} - \ldots \right) \\
&= \cos(x) - i \sin(x)
\end{align*}
\]
~~~

!id: inJ6BD8ofs
!text:
\[
{{c2::\left( \cos(x) + i \sin(x) \right)^n::\ldots}}
=
{{c1::\cos(nx) + i \sin(nx)::\ldots}}
\]

De Moivre's Formula
!back extra:
For \( x \in \mathbb{R} \) and \( n \in \mathbb{Z} \), Euler's formula and the exponential power law give
\[
\begin{align*}
(\cos x + i\sin x)^n
&= (e^{ix})^n \\
&= e^{inx} \\
&= \cos(nx) + i\sin(nx).
\end{align*}
\]
~~~

!id: kBXzrvvubz
!text:
\[
{{c2::z^n = 1::\ldots}}
\iff
{{c1::z = e^{2\pi i k / n}, \quad k = 0, 1, \ldots, n-1::\ldots}}
\]

\( n \)th Roots of Unity
!back extra:
\[
\begin{align*}
z = re^{i\theta}, \ z^n = 1
&\implies r^n e^{in\theta} = e^{i \cdot 2\pi k}, \quad k \in \mathbb{Z} \\
&\implies r = 1, \quad n\theta = 2\pi k \\
&\implies \theta = \frac{2\pi k}{n}
\end{align*}
\]
Taking \( k = 0, 1, \ldots, n-1 \) gives all \( n \) distinct roots; larger \( k \) repeats these by \( 2\pi \)-periodicity.
~~~

!id: HyyVM3fcRE
!text:
For \( r > 0 \) and \( \theta \in \mathbb{R} \), the **complex logarithm** (all values) is given by

\[
{{c2::\ln(re^{i\theta})::\ldots}}
=
{{c1::\left\{ \ln r + i(\theta + 2\pi k) : k \in \mathbb{Z} \right\}::\ldots}}
\]
!back extra:
\[
\begin{align*}
e^{\ln r + i(\theta + 2\pi k)}
&= e^{\ln r}e^{i\theta}e^{2\pi ik} \\
&= re^{i\theta}(\cos(2\pi k) + i \cdot \sin(2\pi k)) \\
&= re^{i\theta}(1 + i \cdot 0) \\
&= re^{i\theta}
\end{align*}
\]
~~~

!id: vqW0Jwvyk8
!text:
For \( r > 0 \) and \( \theta \in \mathbb{R} \) with \( {{c3::-\pi < \theta \le \pi::\text{domain restriction} }} \), the **principal value** of the complex logarithm is

\[
{{c2::\operatorname{Log}(re^{i\theta})::\ldots}}
=
{{c1::\ln r + i\theta::\ldots}}
\]
!back extra: Restricting \( \theta \) to \( (-\pi, \pi] \) picks out a single value of the complex logarithm, making \( \operatorname{Log} \) single-valued, unlike the general complex logarithm \( \ln \), which is multi-valued over all \( k \in \mathbb{Z} \).
~~~
